"Administrative Division « Government of Timor-Leste (https://timor-leste.gov.tl/?lang=en&p=91)\nciteturn363view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://timor-leste.gov.tl/?lang=en&p=91\",\"lineno\":114}); Total lines: 175\nL92: Aileu and Ermera have the highest average number, 11 villages per district, and Ainaro and Oecussi-Ambeno the lowest, with five villages per subdistrict. The most central and mountainous subdistricts with the highest number of villages are: Aileu, in the Aileu district, and Bobonaro, in the Bobonaro district, with 18 divisions each; however, the subdistricts of Hato Udo, in Ainaro, and Tutuala, in Lautem, which lie near the coast, have only two villages each.\nL93: In regards to area, the largest villages lie on the easternmost part of Timor-Leste, specifically Laline in the subdistrict of Lacluta, Viqueque, with 212 km². Dili comprises the smallest villages (15 altogether), similar to neighborhoods (the so called bairos), with areas between 2 km² and 0.06 km^{2}. The population distribution is highly uneven. Among the four villages that constitute the subdistrict of Fatululik, in Cova-Lima, two have a population of less than 500 inhabitants.\nL94: One of these villages has only 135 people, being the least populated village in Timor-Leste. As expected, the villages with high demographic rates (over 5,000 inhabitants per square kilometer) belong to the Dili district, namely to the Dom Aleixo subdistrict. The most populated village is Fuiloro in Lospalos, in the Lautem district, with a population of 10,000 inhabitants.\nL95: ## Brief Description by District\nL96: \nL97: District of Aileucite56†Image: Distrito de Aileu L98: It lies in the highlands to the south of Dili, the national capital. It has a population of 44.325 (Census 2010) and an area of 729 km², with a demographic density of 50,6 h/km². The capital of the district is also named Aileu. This district used to belong to Dili municipality and was only given its autonomy during the last years of the Portuguese administration. Aileu district comprises the subdistricts of Aileu, Laulara, Liquidoe and Remexio.\nL99: \nL100: \nL101: \nL102: Ainaro Districtcite57†Image: Distrito de Ainaro L103: Located in the southwest part of the country. It has a population of 59.175 inhabitants (Census 2010) and an area of 797 km². The capital of the district is also named Ainaro. Ainaro district is identical to that of the same in Portuguese Timor, with the following exceptions: during the Indonesian administration, the subdistrict of Turiscai became part of Manufahi, while the subdistrict of Hatudo became part of Ainaro in exchange.\nL104: Ainaro district comprises today the subdistricts of Ainaro, Hatudo, Hatu Builico and Maubisse. Ainaro has a great abundance of rivers and fertile soil for agriculture. It has a coastal area on the south part of the island, but also mountain ranges, such as the Mount Ramelau (2.960 m) which forms the highest peak in the country. Ainaro played an important role during the Resistance period due to its topographic features which provided shelter for the guerrilla.\nL105: In addition to the official languages of the country (Tetun and Portuguese), the majority of the population speaks Mambai.\nL106: \nL107: \nL108: Baucau Districtcite58†Image: Distrito de Baucau L109: Baucau is located on the eastern part of the country, around 122 km of Dili. It is the second biggest city of the country with a population of 111.694 inhabitants (Census 2010) and an area of 1.494 km². The capital of the district is also named Baucau. During the Portuguese colonization, it was called Vila Salazar, after the Portuguese dictator. Baucau District comprises the subdistricts of Baguia, Baucau, Fatu Maca, Laga, Quelicai, Vemasse and Venilale (formely known as Vila Viçosa).\nL110: Baucau has the country’s biggest international airport (IATA code: NCH) at 6 km from the city, which is now inoperative for commercial flights. The tunnels in the subdistrict of Venilale (ex-Vila Viçosa) are a legacy of the Japanese occupation during the Second World War. The main economic activity of Baucau is agriculture (corn, rice, peanut, coconut and horticultural crops).\nL111: With the gradual improvement of the transport system and the provision of electric power, the private sector and the small and medium enterprises are now registering a progressive development. Baucau offers a wide coastline with attractive beaches, ideal for swimming and other water sports. In addition to the official languages of the country (Tetun and Portuguese), the majority of the population speaks Macassai.\nL112: \nL113: \nL114: Bobonaro Districtcite59†Image: Distrito de Bobonaro L115: It is located on the western part of the country, and borders Indonesia to the west. It has a population of 92.049 inhabitants (Census 2010) and an area of 1.368 km². The capital of Bobonaro is Maliana, 149 km southwest of Dili, the national capital. Bobonaro district is identical to that of the same in Portuguese Timor, with the only difference that its capital was formerly called Vila Armindo Monteiro. The district includes the subdistricts of Atabae, Balibó, Bobonaro, Cailaco, Lolotoi and Maliana.\nL116: Cova Lima Districtcite60†Image: Distrito de Cova Lima L117: \nL118: It is located on the western part of the country and borders Indonesia to the west. It has a population of 59.455 inhabitants (Census 2010) and an area of 1.226 km². The capital of Cova Lima is Suai which lies 136 km southwest of Dili, the national capital. Cova Lima district comprises the subdistricts of Fatululik, Fatumean, Fohorem, Zumulai, Maucatar, Suai and Tilomar.\nL119: \nL120: \nL121: \nL122: Díli District (capital)cite61†Image: Distrito de Díli L123: Lies on the north coast of the island. It borders the districts of Manatuto to the east, Aileu to the south, Liquiça to the west and the Savu Sea to the north; it comprises also Atauro Island to the north. It has a population of 234.026 inhabitants (Census 2010) and an area of 372 km². It is the country’s smallest district. Its capital is the city of Dili which is also the national capital.\nL124: The district is identical to that of the same in Portuguese Timor, with the only difference that it used to comprise the municipality (today district) of Aileu. Dili comprises the subdistricts of Atauro, Cristo-Rei, Dom Aleixo, Nain Feto, Metinaro and Vera Cruz.\nL125: \nL126: \nL127: Ermera Districtcite62†Image: Distrito de Ermera L128: \nL129: Located in the central part of the country. It has a population of 117.064 inhabitants (Census 2010) and an area of 746 km². The capital of Ermera is Gleno, 58 km southwest of Dili, the national capital. Ermera comprises the subdistricts of Atsabe, Ermera, Hatólia, Letefuó and Railaco. Among other agricultural practices, Ermera distinguishes itself as one of the major coffee growing areas.\nL130: \nL131: \nL132: \nL133: Lautém Districtcite63†Image: Distrito de Lautém L134: Lies on the eastern end of Timor Island; it also includes Jaco island. It has a population of 59.787 inhabitants (Census 2010) and an area of 1.702 km². The capital of Lautem is Lospalos, 248 km east of Dili, the national capital. The district is identical to that of the same in Portuguese Timor. At that time, many areas had Portuguese names, such as Vila Nova de Malaca (today Lautem), Nova Nazaré (Com), Nova Sagres (Tutuala), Nova Âncora (Laivai) and Silvícola (Loré), among others.\nL135: Lautem comprises the subdistricts of Iliomar, Lautem Moro, Lospalos, Luro and Tutuala. In addition to the official languages of the country (Tetun and Portuguese), the population speaks Fataluku, a Papuan language spoken in the islands of New Guinea.\nL136: \nL137: \nL138: Liquiçá Districtcite64†Image: Distrito de Liquiçá L139: Located on the northern coast of the country, about 32 km of Dili. It borders the districts of Bobonaro and Ermera to the south; Dili to the east; and the Savu Sea to the northwest. It has a population of 63.403 inhabitants (Census 2010) and an area of 543 km². The capital of the district is also named Liquiça. Liquiça comprises the subdistricts of Bazartete, Liquiça and Maubara. The rocky beach is one of the main attractions for tourists in Liquiça.\nL140: In addition to the official languages of the country (Tetun and Portuguese), the majority of the population speaks Tocodede.\nL141: \nL142: \nL143: Manatuto Districtcite65†Image: Distrito de Manatuto L144: Located in the central part of the country; it reaches both the south and north coasts of the island. It borders the Strait of Wetar to the north; the districts of Baucau and Viqueque to the east; the Timor Sea to the south; and the districts of Manufahi, Aileu and Dili to the west. It has a population of 42.742 inhabitants (Census 2010) and an area of 1.706 km². The capital of the district is also named Manatuto.\nL145: The longest river in Timor-Leste, the Laclo river, reaches the sea near Manatuto, between the Ponta de Subaio and Lanessana bay. Manatuto comprises the subdistricts of Barique-Natarbora, Laclo, Laclubar, Laleia, Manatuto and Soibada. In addition to the official languages (Tetun and Portuguese), the majority of the population speaks Galóli, which is recognized as a “national language” by the Constitution.\nL146: \nL147: \nL148: Manufahi Districtcite66†Image: Distrito de Manufahi L149: Located on the south coast of the island, it borders the district of Manatuto to the east, Aileu to the north, Ainaro to the west and the Timor Sea to the south. It has a population of 48.628 inhabitants (Census 2010) and an area of 1.325 km². The capital of the district is Same. In 1945, during the Portuguese Timor, this district was also called Same. It now comprises the subdistricts of Alas, Fatuberliu, Same and Turiscai.\nL150: The latter – Turiscai, previously in Ainaro, was moved to Manufahi during the Indonesian administration, while Hatudo was split off from Manufahi and joined to Ainaro. In addition to the official languages of the country (Tetun and Portuguese), the majority of the population speaks Mambai.\nL151: \nL152: \nL153: Oecussi-Ambeno Districtcite67†Image: Distrito de Oecussi-Ambeno L154: Located on the north coast of the western part of Timor Island. It is separated from the rest of Timor-Leste by the Indonesian territory which surrounds the small enclave in all directions, except to the north, where it borders the Savu Sea. The district of Oecussi-Ambeno was named after the two original kingdoms which now constitute the district. The territory has 64.025 inhabitants (Census 2010) and an area of 815 km².\nL155: The capital is the city of Pante Macassar, formerly known as Vila Taveiro during the Portuguese administration. The district of Oecussi-Ambeno is identical to that of the same in Portuguese Timor. It was the last Timorese circumscription elevated to district in August 1973. It now includes the subdistricts of Nitibe, Oesilo, Pante Macassar and Passabe.\nL156: Oecussi-Ambeno was the first place on which the Portuguese established themselves at their arrival. This is the reason why the district is considered the cradle of Timor-Leste. In 1556, a group of Dominican brothers established the first village in Lifau, six kilometers to the west of Pante Macassar. In 1702, Lifau became the capital of the colony when it received the first governor from Lisbon.\nL157: It held the status until 1767, when the Portuguese decided to transfer the capital to Dili, as a result of the frequent attacks by the Dutch forces. It was only in 1859, with the Treaty of Lisbon, that Portugal and the Netherlands divided the island: West Timor was given to the Dutch, with its colonial seat in Kupang, and Timor-Leste became Portuguese, with its seat in Dili. Portugal recognized Oecussi-Ambeno as a Portuguese enclave surrounded by Dutch territory.\nL158: The Indonesian invasion of the Portuguese Timor was launched over the western part of the island, namely through Oecussi, on November 29^{th}, 1975, a week before the official invasion. On that day, an Indonesian fifth column raised the Indonesian flag in Pante Macassar. However, even under Indonesian rule, Oecussi-Ambeno continued to be administrated as part of the province of Timor Timur.\nL159: Therefore, following the recognition of the independence of Timor-Leste, in 2002, Oecussi-Ambeno became naturally a segment of the Timorese nation. In addition to the official languages (Tetun and Portuguese), a great part of the population speaks Baikeno.\nL160: \nL161: \nL162: Viqueque Districtcite68†Image: Distrito de Viqueque L163: Located on the south coast of the island. It borders the district of Lautem to the east, Baucau to the north, Manatuto to the west and the Timor Sea to the south. It has a population of 70.036 inhabitants (Census 2010) and an area of 1.781 km². The capital of the district is also named Viqueque. The Viqueque district comprises the subdistricts of Lacluta, Ossu, Uatolari (formely called Leça), Uato Carabau and Viqueque.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAutoridade Munisipal Covalima | Portal Municipal (https://covalima.gov.tl/)\nciteturn363view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://covalima.gov.tl/\",\"lineno\":100}); Total lines: 493\nL0: cite0†Image: Miguel Armada Cardoso L1: \nL2: Miguel Armada Cardoso\nL3: \nL4: ## Autoridade Munisipal Covalima\nL5: \nL6: cite0†Image: Miguel Armada Cardoso L7: \nL8: Miguel Armada Cardoso\nL9: \nL10: “ Fasil, Lais no Amigavel ”\nL11: \nL12:   * cite1†Visaun no Misaun L13:   * cite2†Membru Konsellu L14:   * cite3†Oportunidade Serbisu L15:   * cite4†Serbisu Munisipal L16:   * cite5†Balkaun Uniku†portal.municipio.gov.tl L17: \nL18: ### Eventus cite6†Hare hotu L19: \nL20:   * Jan 01\nL21: \nL22: ###### cite7†ATIVIDADE SECAM ASOC PERIODU 2026 L23: \nL24:   * Dec 10\nL25: ###### cite8†SERIMONIA INAGURASAUN EDIFISIU BALKAUN UNIKU IHA MUNISIPIU COVALIMA L26: \nL27: Santa Rosa, Aldeia Tabako Lot, Suco Debos, PA. Suai\nL28: \nL29:   * Jan 01\nL30: \nL31: ###### cite9†Formasaun kona-ba Jestaun Baze de Dados iha Salaun AM Covalima L32: ### Notísias resentes cite10†Hare hotu L33: \nL34:   * ###### cite11†𝐌𝐀𝐄 𝐇𝐚𝐥𝐚’𝐨 𝐓𝐫𝐚𝐢𝐧𝐢𝐧𝐠 𝐛𝐚 𝐏𝐨𝐧𝐭u F𝐨kal 𝐌𝐮𝐧𝐢𝐬𝐢𝐩𝐢𝐮𝐬 𝐢𝐧𝐤𝐥𝐮𝐢 𝐀𝐭𝐚𝐮𝐫𝐨 𝐧𝐨 𝐑𝐀𝐄𝐎𝐀 L35: \nL36: Post iha: 9 Oct 2026\nL37: \nL38:   * ###### cite12†PROGRAMA APOIU DEZENVOLVIMENTU (PARTISIPA) ORGANIZA FORMASAUN KONA-BA ABORDAJEN JÉNERU, LIDERANSA, NO DESENTRALIZASAUN PODÉR LOKÁL BA FETO POTENSIÁL L39: \nL40: Post iha: 6 Oct 2026\nL41: \nL42:   * ###### cite13†ENKONTRU REGULAR L43: \nL44: Post iha: 6 Oct 2026\nL45: \nL46:   * ###### cite14†Monitorizasaun no Avaliasaun ba Planu Dezenvolvimentu Munisipál L47: \nL48: Post iha: 29 Sep 2026\nL49:   * ###### cite15†KJDM Covalima Entrega Apoiu Emerjénsia ba Família ne’ebé Sira-nia Uma Ahi Han iha Suku Beco L50: \nL51: Post iha: 29 Sep 2026\nL52: ### Dokumentus cite16†Hare hotu L53: \nL54:   * ###### cite17†Rezultadu husi Monitorizasaun ba atividades Programa Merenda Eskola L55: \nL56:     * Agência Municipal de Planeamento\nL57: Post iha: 23 Jun 2024\nL58:   * ###### cite18†Exekusaun Projetu PDIM Ate Maio 2025 L59: \nL60:     * PDIM\nL61: Post iha: 9 Jun 2025\nL62:   * ###### cite19†Aprejentasaun Relatoriu Trimestral Q1-Q2 Tinan 2025 L63:     * Agência Municipal de Planeamento\nL64: Post iha: 14 Jun 2025\nL65:   * ###### cite20†Slide Aprejentasaun Planu Assaun Annual 2025 ho Prusentu (%) Exekusaun Orsamentu Servisu Munisipal Munisipiu Covalima iha Q2 (Abril – Junho 2025) L66: \nL67:     * Agência Municipal de Planeamento\nL68: Post iha: 14 Jun 2025\nL69:   * ###### cite21†Anúnsiu Selesaun Empreza iha Konkursu Públiku Municipál Munisipiu Covalima L70: \nL71:     * Serviço Municipal de Aprovisionamento\nL72: Post iha: 14 Jun 2025\nL73: \nL74:   * cite22†Image L75: \nL76: Populasaun\nL77: \nL78: 90,181\nL79: \nL80:   * cite23†Image L81: \nL82: Área\nL83: 1206.6 km^{2}\nL84: \nL85:   * cite24†Image L86: \nL87: Lian\nL88: \nL89: Tétum terik, Bunak, Kemak\nL90: \nL91:   * cite25†Image L92: \nL93: Kapital\nL94: \nL95: Suai\nL96: ## Miguel Armada Cardoso | Presidente Autoridade do Municipio Covalima\nL97: \nL98: “Se suas ações criam um legado que inspira outros a sonhar mais, aprender mais, fazer mais e se tornarem mais, então você é um grande líder.”\nL99: Miguel Armada Cardoso, Lic. Eco natural husi Munisípiu Covalima, atualmente assumi kargo hanesan Presidente Autoridade do Municipio de Covalima. Miguel Armada Cardoso, Lic. Eco ho Nível de Estudo Licenciatura no iha habilidade profissional hanesan tuir mai : ABILIDADE ADISIONAL SIRA 1. Abilidade Fleksivilidade atu Serviso iha Kualker Situasaun. 2. Abilidade diak iha Serviso Mobilizasaun Komunidade ba Asaun Mudansa Sosial. 3. Bele Serviso Konaba Monitorizasaun no Evaluasaun ba Programa Organizasaun.\nL100: cite26†Hare Perfil L101: \nL102: Jeografia\nL103: Covalima mak hanesan munisípiu ida husi Timor-Leste, lokalizado iha parte este, ne’ebé halo fronteira ho Munisípiu Ainaro (este), Tasi Mane (sul), Indonésia (oeste) no Munisípiu Bobonaro (norte). Munisípiu referido iha postu administrativu 7 : Fatululic, Fatumea, Fohorem, Maucatar, Suai, Tilomar no Zumalai, ne’ebé inklui 30 suku.\nL104: Demográfikamente, Covalima iha total de populasaun hamutuk 68,863 (projesaun 2019) no densidade populasaun mak 57,06 ba área 1,206.66 km2. Grupu de populasaun ida ne’e, koalia lian : Tetun, Tetun Terik, Bunak no Kemak.\nL105: * * *\nL106: \nL107: Toponomia\nL108: Hanesan mós ho munisípiu seluk, Covalima mós iha signifikadu próprio ba naran munisípiu nian. Tuir istória ne’ebé haktuir husi ema Suai katak, etimologikamente “Covalima” mai husi liafuan tetun terik, ne’ebé signifika: Coba lima, “Coba” kata dadebuk ka fatin tau mama (malus, bua), no “lima” signika liurai nian oan feto nain lima husi Reino Fohoren Nutetuk.\nL109: Maibe tuir aspektu ukun nain ka autoridade tradisional “Covalima” reprezenta liurai lima ne’ebé ukun iha area covalima hanesan: Reino Camnasa, Fohoren, Suai, Maucatar no Reino Taromana.\nL110: * * *\nL111: \nL112: Istoria\nL113: Tuir istória, antes Portugues sira estabelese sira nia autoridade iha rai Covalima, iha rai ne’e iha ona autoridade tardicional ne’ebé ukun . Quando Governo portugues introdús sira nia autoridade, liu husi Governador Antonio Coelho Guerreiro iha tinan 1698, komeça halo intervensaun ba autoridade tradisional sira iha Timor inklui iha rai Covalima.\nL114: Iha loron 20 de Abril 1859, liu husi Governador ne rasik estabelese 11 Distritu, Covalima/Camnassa no Raimean mos sai hanesan reino ne’ebé hola parte iha Distritu de Dotik ho nia sede iha Alas. Liu tiha segunda Guerra Mundial, Governador Oscar Ruas muda komando militar ne’e transforma fali ba Circuscriação Civil ho nia sede iha Fohoren, maibe tanma tanba ekonomia la permite, liu tiha tinan hira, Covalima tama hamutuk fali ho Distritu Bobonaro.\nL115: Iha tempu okupasaun indonesia, Covalima mantem hanesan Distritu no ikus mai sai munisípiu.\nL116: * * *\nL117: \nL118: Klima\nL119: \nL120: Em termos de klima, fahe ba epoka rua : epoka bai-loron, ne’ebé hahu husi fulan julho too setembro no epoka udan hahu husi novembro to junho. Rejistu udan hatudu katak, iha outubro iha temperatura aas, 29c no iha setembro iha temperatura ne’ebé ki’ik liu, 12c. Mudansa klimatika afeta maka’as iha Covalima. Iha tempu bai-loron, provoca rai manas, liu-liu iha area kostal nune’e afeta ba preservasaun be’e menus, no wainhira rai maran no tempu udan, acontece inundasaun.\nL121: \nL122: * * *\nL123: \nL124: Pontu Interese\nL125: Pontu interese husi munisípiu Covalima mak : Tasi Suai Loro, Fonte de gás, etc\nL126: \nL127: * * *\nL128: \nL129: Karakteristiku Terenu :\nL130: \nL131: Munisípiu Covalima iha karakterístiku terenu ne’ebé oioin, kompostu husi foho, planaltu, no mós tasi-ibun ne’ebé luan. Karakterístiku jeográfiku sira-ne’e fahe ba kategoria balu tuir mai ne’e:\nL132: \nL133:   1. Zona Foho\nL134: \nL135: Parte norte husi munisípiu ne’e, ne’ebé halo fronteira ho Bobonaro no Indonézia (West Timor), domina ho foho ne’ebé aas no luan.\nL136: Topografia: Terenu ne’ebé nakdulas, lerek, no rai-lolon ne’ebé aas.\nL137: \nL138: Vantajen: Fatin ne’e apropriadu tebes ba agrikultura nian, liuliu ba kafé no ai-fuan sira ne’ebé presiza klima malirin.\nL139: \nL140: 2. Zona Tasi-Ibun (Plains of the South Coast)\nL141: \nL142: Covalima konhesidu ho ninia kosta súl ne’ebé luan no mahar, liuliu iha área Suai no mós arredores.\nL143: \nL144: Terenu: Rai-tetuk ne’ebé luan (lowlands).\nL145: Potensiál: Área ne’e mak sai sentru ba produsaun hare (natara) tanba rai-tetuk ne’ebé bo’ot. Alende ne’e, iha mós potensiál boot ba dezenvolvimentu infraestrutura tanba terenu ne’ebé la nakdulas.\nL146: \nL147: 3. Hidrografia (Mota)\nL148: \nL149: Terenu iha Covalima mós influensiadu husi mota boot balu ne’ebé suli husi norte ba súl, hanesan:\nL150: \nL151: Mota Tafara: Mota ne’ebé importante tebes ba irrigasaun no vida moris populasaun nian.\nL152: Karakterístiku: Iha tempu udan, mota sira-ne’e bele lori bee-boot (be-sa’e) tanba volume bee ne’ebé boot husi foho sira.\nL153: \nL154: 4. Rekursu Naturál no Jeolojia\nL155: \nL156: Terenu Covalima nian mós iha karakterístiku jeolójiku ne’ebé úniku:\nL157: \nL158: Mina-Rai (Petróleo & Gás): Iha área balu iha tasi-ibun, iha fatin ne’ebé mina suli sai rasik husi rai (natural oil seeps), ne’ebé hatudu potensiál rekursu minerais ne’ebé boot iha terenu laran.\nL159: \nL160: * * *\nL161: \nL162: Divizaun no Luan Area Area Administrativa\nL163: Municípiu Covalima mak munisípiu ida ne’ebe iha Timor-Leste ne’ebé lokaliza iha parte sudoeste, baliza ho Indonézia. Tuir rejime jurídiku ba Administrasaun Lokál no estrutura administrativa iha Covalima fahe ba Postu Administrativu no Suku sira.\nL164: \nL165: Tuir mai mak detallu kona-ba divizaun no luan área nian:\nL166: \nL167: Divizaun Administrativa\nL168: \nL169: Municípiu Covalima kompostu husi 7 Postu Administrativu no 30 Suku. Sidade kapitál ba munisípiu ne’e mak Suai.\nL170: \nL171: Lista Postu Administrativu sira mak:\nL172: \nL173: Suai (Kapitál)\nL174:   * Zumalai\nL175:   * Fatumea\nL176:   * Fatululic\nL177:   * Maucatar\nL178:   * Tilomar\nL179:   * Fohorem\nL180: \nL181: * * *\nL182: \nL183: Luan Área (Superfísie) :\nL184: \nL185: Luan área totál ba Municípiu Covalima mak maizumenus 1206.6 km^{2}\nL186: \nL187: * * *\nL188: \nL189: Karaterístika Territóriu\nL190:   * Zona Fronteira: Postu sira hanesan Fatumea, Fatululic, Tilomar, no Fohorem halo baliza direta ho Provínsia Nusa Tenggara Timur (NTT), Indonézia.\nL191:   * Balkaun Úniku no Portal: Atu fasilita jestaun iha área hirak ne’e, Governu foti ona inisiativa hodi dezenvolve servisu integradu hanesan “Balkaun Úniku” atu aselera atendimentu ba komunidade iha nível munisipal.\nL192:   * Poténsia Ekonómika: Área luan Covalima nian uza barak ba agrikultura, pekuária, no mós sai hanesan sentru estratéjiku ba setór petróleu no gás (Supply Base).\nL193: * * *\nL194: ## Postu administrativu\nL195: \nL196: ## cite27†ZUMALAI L197: \nL198: ## cite28†MAUCATAR L199: \nL200: ## cite29†FOHOREM L201: \nL202: ## cite30†SUAI L203: \nL204: ## cite31†FATULULIC L205: \nL206: ## cite32†FATUMEA L207: \nL208: ## cite33†TILOMAR L209: \nL210: ## Explora no kunhese munisípiu\nL211: \nL212: cite34†Image: Hahan/Restaurante L213: \nL214: ### cite35†Hahan/Restaurante L215: \nL216: cite36†Image: Oinsa to’o fatin L217: \nL218: ### cite37†Oinsa to’o fatin L219: \nL220: cite38†Image: Saida mak bele halo L221: \nL222: ### cite39†Saida mak bele halo L223: \nL224: cite40†Image: Saida mak bele kunhese L225: \nL226: ### cite41†Saida mak bele kunhese L227: \nL228:   1. A\nL229: #### cite42†Secretário Municipal d